============================================================
1. CSS BASICS
============================================================

CSS = Cascading Style Sheets

CSS is a stylesheet language used to control the presentation
and appearance of HTML elements.

CSS can control things such as:

- colors
- fonts
- backgrounds
- spacing
- dimensions
- layouts
- alignment
- visual presentation


------------------------------------------------------------
BASIC ANATOMY OF A CSS RULE
------------------------------------------------------------

A CSS rule normally contains:

1. Selector
2. Declaration block

Example:

p {
    color: blue;
}

p
= selector

color
= property

blue
= value

color: blue;
= declaration


General syntax:

selector {
    property: value;
}


Multiple declarations:

p {
    color: blue;
    font-size: 18px;
}


The selector determines WHAT gets styled.

The declarations determine HOW it gets styled.


============================================================
2. META VIEWPORT
============================================================

A common viewport meta element is:

<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">

It tells the browser how the page should be sized and scaled,
especially on mobile devices.


width=device-width

Sets the viewport width to the width of the device.


initial-scale=1.0

Sets the initial zoom level.


A typical HTML head might contain:

<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport"
          content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
    <title>Page Title</title>
</head>


============================================================
3. DEFAULT BROWSER STYLES
============================================================

Browsers automatically apply default styles to HTML elements.

These are often called:

user-agent styles
or
user-agent stylesheet rules


Examples:

- headings have default font sizes
- paragraphs have default margins
- links are normally underlined
- links normally have their own colors
- lists normally have indentation
- body may have a default margin


Example:

<h1>Hello</h1>

Even without your own CSS, the browser makes the h1:

- large
- bold
- block-level
- spaced from surrounding content


Your CSS can override browser defaults.


============================================================
4. INLINE, INTERNAL, AND EXTERNAL CSS
============================================================


------------------------------------------------------------
INLINE CSS
------------------------------------------------------------

Inline CSS is written directly on an HTML element using
the style attribute.

Example:

<p style="color: red;">Hello</p>


Advantages:

- useful for very small tests
- applies directly to a specific element


Disadvantages:

- mixes HTML structure with presentation
- difficult to maintain
- difficult to reuse
- usually avoided in larger projects


------------------------------------------------------------
INTERNAL CSS
------------------------------------------------------------

Internal CSS is written inside a style element,
usually inside the HTML head.

Example:

<head>

    <style>
        p {
            color: red;
        }
    </style>

</head>


Useful for:

- small demonstrations
- single-page examples
- quick experiments


------------------------------------------------------------
EXTERNAL CSS
------------------------------------------------------------

External CSS is stored in a separate .css file.

Example file:

styles.css


HTML connects to it using:

<link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">


Example:

<head>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
</head>


External stylesheets are normally preferred for projects
because they provide better separation of concerns.

HTML
= structure and meaning

CSS
= presentation and styling


IMPORTANT:

Internal CSS is NOT automatically more specific than
external CSS.

Specificity comes primarily from selectors.

If competing rules have equal specificity, source order
can determine which declaration wins.


============================================================
5. WIDTH AND HEIGHT
============================================================


------------------------------------------------------------
WIDTH
------------------------------------------------------------

width controls the width of an element.

Example:

div {
    width: 300px;
}


If width is not explicitly set, its default value is:

auto


The browser determines the width based on factors such as:

- content
- containing element
- display type


------------------------------------------------------------
MIN-WIDTH
------------------------------------------------------------

min-width establishes the smallest width an element
should normally become.

Example:

div {
    min-width: 200px;
}


------------------------------------------------------------
MAX-WIDTH
------------------------------------------------------------

max-width establishes the largest width an element
should normally become.

Example:

div {
    max-width: 800px;
}


max-width is especially useful for keeping content from
becoming excessively wide.

Example:

.container {
    width: 80%;
    max-width: 800px;
}


------------------------------------------------------------
HEIGHT
------------------------------------------------------------

height controls an element's height.

Example:

div {
    height: 200px;
}


The default height is generally:

auto


This allows the element to grow according to its content.


------------------------------------------------------------
MIN-HEIGHT
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

div {
    min-height: 200px;
}


The element can become taller if necessary.


------------------------------------------------------------
MAX-HEIGHT
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

div {
    max-height: 500px;
}


This limits how tall the element can normally become.


============================================================
6. CSS COMBINATORS
============================================================

Combinators select elements based on relationships
between elements in the HTML structure.


------------------------------------------------------------
DESCENDANT COMBINATOR
------------------------------------------------------------

Syntax:

A B


Example:

nav a {
    color: green;
}


Meaning:

Select every a element located somewhere inside nav.


Example HTML:

<nav>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <a href="#">Home</a>
        </li>
    </ul>
</nav>


The link does NOT need to be a direct child of nav.

It only needs to be a descendant.


Think:

"Find B somewhere inside A."


------------------------------------------------------------
CHILD COMBINATOR
------------------------------------------------------------

Symbol:

>


Syntax:

A > B


Example:

section > p {
    color: blue;
}


Meaning:

Select p elements that are DIRECT children of section.


Example:

<section>

    <p>This matches.</p>

    <div>
        <p>This does not match.</p>
    </div>

</section>


The second paragraph is a descendant of section,
but it is NOT a direct child.


Think:

"Find B immediately inside A."


------------------------------------------------------------
NEXT-SIBLING / ADJACENT-SIBLING COMBINATOR
------------------------------------------------------------

Symbol:

+


Syntax:

A + B


Example:

h2 + p {
    color: red;
}


Meaning:

Select a p element that immediately follows an h2
and shares the same parent.


Example:

<h2>Heading</h2>
<p>This matches.</p>
<p>This does not match the h2 + p rule.</p>


Only the first paragraph immediately following the h2
matches.


Think:

"Find the B immediately after A."


------------------------------------------------------------
SUBSEQUENT-SIBLING COMBINATOR
------------------------------------------------------------

Symbol:

~


Syntax:

A ~ B


Example:

h2 ~ p {
    color: green;
}


Meaning:

Select matching B elements that:

- share the same parent as A
- appear after A


Example:

<h2>Heading</h2>

<p>This matches.</p>
<div>Something else</div>
<p>This also matches.</p>


Both paragraphs occur after the h2 and share its parent.


Think:

"Find matching B siblings appearing later than A."


============================================================
7. INLINE, BLOCK, AND INLINE-BLOCK
============================================================


------------------------------------------------------------
INLINE ELEMENTS
------------------------------------------------------------

Inline elements participate within a line of text.

They normally:

- do not start on a new line
- use only the horizontal space they need
- remain in the surrounding text flow


Common examples:

span
a
img


Example:

<p>
    Hello <span>world</span> today.
</p>


The span stays inside the line.


Normal width and height rules do not behave on ordinary
inline elements the same way they do on block elements.


------------------------------------------------------------
BLOCK-LEVEL ELEMENTS
------------------------------------------------------------

Block-level elements normally:

- start on a new line
- take the available width by default
- accept width and height


Common examples:

div
p
section


Example:

<div>First</div>
<div>Second</div>


The two div elements normally appear on separate lines.


------------------------------------------------------------
INLINE-BLOCK
------------------------------------------------------------

An element can be given:

display: inline-block;


Example:

span {
    display: inline-block;
    width: 150px;
    height: 75px;
}


inline-block combines useful characteristics of both
inline and block behavior.


INLINE:

- stays within the line
- width/height do not normally control it


BLOCK:

- starts on a new line
- width/height work


INLINE-BLOCK:

- can remain beside other elements
- width works
- height works


Quick comparison:

INLINE
Same line: YES
Width/height: generally NO

BLOCK
Same line: NO
Width/height: YES

INLINE-BLOCK
Same line: YES
Width/height: YES


============================================================
8. MARGIN AND PADDING
============================================================


------------------------------------------------------------
PADDING
------------------------------------------------------------

Padding is space INSIDE an element.

It exists between:

content
and
the element's edge/border


Example:

div {
    padding: 20px;
}


Think:

CONTENT
↓
PADDING
↓
EDGE / BORDER


Padding pushes the content inward.


------------------------------------------------------------
MARGIN
------------------------------------------------------------

Margin is space OUTSIDE an element.

It separates an element from surrounding elements.

Example:

div {
    margin: 20px;
}


Think:

MARGIN
↓
ELEMENT
↓
PADDING
↓
CONTENT


Margin pushes surrounding elements away.


IMPORTANT:

Margin is generally NOT inherited.

If a parent has:

section {
    margin: 30px;
}

its children move visually because they are inside the
parent element.

That does NOT mean the children inherited margin: 30px.


============================================================
9. MARGIN SHORTHAND
============================================================


ONE VALUE:

margin: 20px;

Means:

top    = 20px
right  = 20px
bottom = 20px
left   = 20px


TWO VALUES:

margin: 10px 20px;

Means:

top/bottom = 10px
left/right = 20px


THREE VALUES:

margin: 10px 20px 30px;

Means:

top        = 10px
left/right = 20px
bottom     = 30px


FOUR VALUES:

margin: 10px 20px 30px 40px;

Order:

top
right
bottom
left


Mnemonic:

TRBL

Top
Right
Bottom
Left


============================================================
10. PADDING SHORTHAND
============================================================

Padding follows the same shorthand pattern.


ONE VALUE:

padding: 20px;


All four sides = 20px


TWO VALUES:

padding: 10px 20px;


top/bottom = 10px
left/right = 20px


THREE VALUES:

padding: 10px 20px 30px;


top        = 10px
left/right = 20px
bottom     = 30px


FOUR VALUES:

padding: 10px 20px 30px 40px;


top    = 10px
right  = 20px
bottom = 30px
left   = 40px


============================================================
11. CSS SPECIFICITY
============================================================

Specificity helps determine which CSS declaration wins
when multiple selectors target the same element/property.


A useful specificity notation is:

INLINE - ID - CLASS - TYPE


Examples:

Universal selector:

*

Specificity:

0-0-0-0


Type selector:

p

Specificity:

0-0-0-1


Class selector:

.notice

Specificity:

0-0-1-0


ID selector:

#important

Specificity:

0-1-0-0


Inline style:

style="color: red;"

Specificity:

1-0-0-0


Simplified ranking:

*
<
type
<
class
<
ID
<
inline style


============================================================
12. UNIVERSAL SELECTOR
============================================================

The universal selector is:

*


Example:

* {
    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}


It matches every element.


Its specificity contribution is:

0-0-0-0


This means it is very broad in what it matches,
but very weak in specificity.


Example:

* {
    color: gray;
}

p {
    color: blue;
}


A paragraph becomes blue because the type selector has
greater specificity.


============================================================
13. TYPE SELECTOR SPECIFICITY
============================================================

A type selector targets HTML elements by element name.

Examples:

p
h1
section
a
div


Example:

p {
    color: blue;
}


Specificity:

0-0-0-1


Type selectors have more specificity than the universal
selector but less than classes and IDs.


============================================================
14. CLASS SELECTOR SPECIFICITY
============================================================

Class selectors begin with:

.


Example:

.notice {
    color: green;
}


Specificity:

0-0-1-0


A class selector normally beats a type selector.


Example:

p {
    color: blue;
}

.notice {
    color: green;
}


HTML:

<p class="notice">Message</p>


Result:

green


============================================================
15. ID SELECTOR SPECIFICITY
============================================================

ID selectors begin with:

#


Example:

#important {
    color: red;
}


Specificity:

0-1-0-0


An ID selector normally beats:

- type selectors
- class selectors


Example:

p {
    color: blue;
}

.message {
    color: green;
}

#featured {
    color: red;
}


HTML:

<p id="featured" class="message">Hello</p>


Result:

red


============================================================
16. INLINE STYLE SPECIFICITY
============================================================

Example:

<p style="color: orange;">Hello</p>


Inline styles are applied directly to the element.

In the specificity model used here:

1-0-0-0


They normally override ordinary:

- type selectors
- class selectors
- ID selectors


Example:

#message {
    color: red;
}


<p id="message" style="color: orange;">Hello</p>


Result:

orange


============================================================
17. !IMPORTANT
============================================================

Example:

.warning {
    color: orange !important;
}


!important changes the priority of a declaration in
the cascade.


Example:

#message {
    color: purple;
}

.warning {
    color: orange !important;
}


HTML:

<p id="message" class="warning">Warning</p>


Result:

orange


IMPORTANT CONCEPT:

!important is NOT simply "extra specificity."

It changes cascade priority.


Best practice:

Use !important sparingly.


Avoid using it simply because:

"My CSS isn't working."


Instead investigate:

- selector matching
- specificity
- cascade
- source order
- inheritance
- DevTools


Overusing !important can make CSS:

- harder to understand
- harder to override
- harder to debug
- harder to maintain


============================================================
18. THE CASCADE ALGORITHM
============================================================

The cascade determines which CSS declaration should
apply when multiple declarations compete.


A useful high-level mental model:

1. RELEVANCE
2. ORIGIN
3. IMPORTANCE
4. SPECIFICITY
5. ORDER OF APPEARANCE


------------------------------------------------------------
1. RELEVANCE
------------------------------------------------------------

Does the rule even apply to the element?

Example:

.not-used {
    color: pink;
}


If no element has class="not-used", the rule is irrelevant.


------------------------------------------------------------
2. ORIGIN
------------------------------------------------------------

CSS can come from different origins.

Examples include:

- browser/user-agent styles
- author styles


Browser example:

Links receive default browser styling.


Author example:

a {
    color: green;
}


Your author CSS can override normal browser defaults.


------------------------------------------------------------
3. IMPORTANCE
------------------------------------------------------------

Important declarations receive different cascade priority.

Example:

color: orange !important;


------------------------------------------------------------
4. SPECIFICITY
------------------------------------------------------------

If competing declarations are otherwise eligible,
selector specificity can determine the winner.


Example:

p
vs.
.notice
vs.
#important


Normally:

ID > CLASS > TYPE


------------------------------------------------------------
5. ORDER OF APPEARANCE
------------------------------------------------------------

When competing declarations have equal priority
and equal specificity, the later rule wins.


Example:

.status {
    color: purple;
}

.status {
    color: teal;
}


Result:

teal


Both selectors have identical specificity.

The second declaration appears later.


IMPORTANT:

Internal CSS does not automatically beat external CSS
because it has "more specificity."

If two matching selectors have the same specificity,
the cascade and source order may determine the winner.


============================================================
19. CSS INHERITANCE
============================================================

Inheritance allows certain CSS property values to pass
from parent elements to descendants.


Example:

body {
    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
    color: darkslategray;
}


Elements inside body can inherit:

font-family
color


You do not need:

h1 {
    font-family: Arial;
}

p {
    font-family: Arial;
}

a {
    font-family: Arial;
}


when the property can naturally inherit from a parent.


------------------------------------------------------------
INHERITED PROPERTY EXAMPLE
------------------------------------------------------------

<section class="workshop">

    <h2>Workshop</h2>

    <p>Hello</p>

</section>


CSS:

.workshop {
    color: darkblue;
}


The h2 and p can inherit:

darkblue


------------------------------------------------------------
DIRECT DECLARATION OVERRIDES INHERITANCE
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

.workshop {
    color: darkblue;
}

.workshop a {
    color: darkgreen;
}


The link receives darkgreen because it has its own
matching declaration rather than simply using the
inherited darkblue value.


------------------------------------------------------------
NOT ALL PROPERTIES INHERIT
------------------------------------------------------------

Commonly inherited properties include:

color
font-family


Properties that generally do NOT inherit include:

margin
padding
width
height


Example:

.parent {
    margin: 30px;
}


The children do not automatically receive:

margin: 30px;


The parent moves, and its children move with it because
they are contained inside the parent.


