LISTS, LINKS, CSS BACKGROUNDS AND BORDERS REVIEW
============================================================


============================================================
1. STYLING LISTS
============================================================


------------------------------------------------------------
LINE-HEIGHT
------------------------------------------------------------

line-height controls the amount of vertical space between
lines of text.

Example:

li {
    line-height: 1.5;
}


Possible values include:

normal
numbers
percentages
length units


Examples:

line-height: normal;

line-height: 1.5;

line-height: 150%;

line-height: 1.5em;


IMPORTANT:

line-height controls spacing BETWEEN LINES OF TEXT.

It does not create external space around the element.


------------------------------------------------------------
MARGIN FOR LIST SPACING
------------------------------------------------------------

Margins can also be used to improve list readability.

Example:

li {
    margin-bottom: 10px;
}


margin-bottom creates space OUTSIDE the list item.

This means:

line-height
→ controls spacing between lines within text

margin
→ controls spacing around/between elements


Example:

li + li {
    margin-top: 10px;
}


This adds space above every li that immediately follows
another li.


============================================================
2. LIST-STYLE-TYPE
============================================================

list-style-type controls the marker shown beside a list item.


Example:

ul {
    list-style-type: square;
}


Possible values include:

disc
circle
square
decimal


Example:

ul {
    list-style-type: circle;
}


For an ordered list:

ol {
    list-style-type: decimal;
}


============================================================
3. LIST-STYLE-POSITION
============================================================

list-style-position controls where the marker appears
relative to the list item's content.


Values:

inside
outside


Example:

ul {
    list-style-position: inside;
}


OUTSIDE:

The marker sits outside the main content box.

This is the default behavior.


INSIDE:

The marker becomes part of the content flow.


Example:

ul {
    list-style-position: inside;
}


This difference becomes easier to see when a list item
wraps onto multiple lines.


============================================================
4. LIST-STYLE-IMAGE
============================================================

list-style-image lets you use an image as the list marker.


Example:

ul {
    list-style-image: url("star.png");
}


The url() function points to the marker image.


Example with a relative path:

ul {
    list-style-image: url("assets/star-marker.png");
}


IMPORTANT:

The source image should generally already be an appropriate
size for use as a list marker.


============================================================
5. LIST-STYLE SHORTHAND
============================================================

The list-style property is shorthand for several list
properties.


It can combine values such as:

list-style-type
list-style-position
list-style-image


Example:

ol {
    list-style: upper-roman inside;
}


Values in the shorthand are separated by spaces.


============================================================
6. STYLING LINKS
============================================================

A pseudo-class is added to a selector to style an element
based on a particular state.


Common link pseudo-classes:

:link
:visited
:hover
:focus
:active


============================================================
7. :LINK
============================================================

:link targets links that the user has NOT visited.


Example:

a:link {
    color: blue;
}


============================================================
8. :VISITED
============================================================

:visited targets links that the user has already visited.


Example:

a:visited {
    color: purple;
}


This gives users a visual indication that they have already
visited a destination.


============================================================
9. :HOVER
============================================================

:hover applies while the pointer is positioned over
an element.


Example:

a:hover {
    color: green;
}


Hover styles help communicate that an element is
interactive.


============================================================
10. :FOCUS
============================================================

:focus applies when an element receives keyboard or
programmatic focus.


Example:

a:focus {
    outline: 2px solid orange;
}


Focus styling is especially important for keyboard users.


Do not remove a focus indicator unless you replace it with
another clearly visible focus style.


============================================================
11. :ACTIVE
============================================================

:active applies while an element is being activated.


For a link, this is commonly the brief period while the
mouse button is being pressed.


Example:

a:active {
    color: red;
}


============================================================
12. COMMON LINK STATE ORDER
============================================================

A common ordering is:

a:link
a:visited
a:hover
a:focus
a:active


A commonly remembered sequence is:

LVHA

Link
Visited
Hover
Active


Focus is often included alongside the interactive states
for keyboard accessibility.


============================================================
13. DEFAULT LINK STYLES
============================================================

Default browser link styles are useful because they help
users recognize interactive text.


Typical browser behavior includes:

- different colors for visited/unvisited links
- underlined links
- visible focus indicators


Custom styles are fine, but links should remain recognizable
as interactive elements.


============================================================
14. BACKGROUND-IMAGE
============================================================

background-image applies an image to an element's
background.


Example:

section {
    background-image: url("background.jpg");
}


The image is painted behind the element's content.


background-image can also accept generated images such as:

linear-gradient()

radial-gradient()


Example:

section {
    background-image: linear-gradient(blue, white);
}


============================================================
15. BACKGROUND-SIZE
============================================================

background-size controls the rendered size of a background
image.


Common values:

cover
contain
auto
length values
percentage values


------------------------------------------------------------
COVER
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

section {
    background-size: cover;
}


cover scales the image so the entire element is covered.

The image keeps its aspect ratio.

IMPORTANT:

Part of the image may be CROPPED.


Mental model:

cover
→ fill the entire container
→ cropping may occur


------------------------------------------------------------
CONTAIN
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

section {
    background-size: contain;
}


contain scales the image so the entire image fits inside
the available area.

The image keeps its aspect ratio.

This may leave unused space in the container.


Mental model:

contain
→ show the entire image
→ empty space may remain


------------------------------------------------------------
AUTO
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

section {
    background-size: auto;
}


auto uses the image's natural proportions.


------------------------------------------------------------
LENGTH / PERCENTAGE
------------------------------------------------------------

Examples:

background-size: 300px;

background-size: 100%;

background-size: 100% 50%;


These values explicitly control the dimensions used for
the background.


============================================================
16. BACKGROUND-REPEAT
============================================================

background-repeat controls whether a background image
tiles across an element.


The default behavior is:

repeat


Example:

section {
    background-repeat: repeat;
}


This repeats the image horizontally and vertically.


Other useful values include:

no-repeat
repeat-x
repeat-y


------------------------------------------------------------
NO-REPEAT
------------------------------------------------------------

section {
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
}


The image appears once.


------------------------------------------------------------
REPEAT-X
------------------------------------------------------------

section {
    background-repeat: repeat-x;
}


Repeats horizontally.


------------------------------------------------------------
REPEAT-Y
------------------------------------------------------------

section {
    background-repeat: repeat-y;
}


Repeats vertically.


Small pattern images are often useful with repeat.

Large photographs usually use no-repeat.


============================================================
17. BACKGROUND-POSITION
============================================================

background-position controls where a background image is
placed inside its element.


Keyword examples:

top
bottom
left
right
center


Example:

section {
    background-position: center;
}


Two values can control horizontal and vertical positioning.


Example:

section {
    background-position: right center;
}


This means:

horizontal = right
vertical   = center


Other examples:

background-position: top left;

background-position: center center;

background-position: bottom right;


Positions can also use:

length values
percentages


============================================================
18. BACKGROUND-ATTACHMENT
============================================================

background-attachment controls how the background behaves
when scrolling.


Important values:

scroll
fixed


------------------------------------------------------------
SCROLL
------------------------------------------------------------

background-attachment: scroll;


This is the normal/default behavior.

The background moves as the page scrolls.


------------------------------------------------------------
FIXED
------------------------------------------------------------

background-attachment: fixed;


The background stays fixed relative to the viewport while
the page content moves.


Quick comparison:

scroll
→ background moves with the page

fixed
→ background stays fixed to the viewport


============================================================
19. BACKGROUND SHORTHAND
============================================================

background is a shorthand property that can combine several
background properties.


Instead of writing multiple declarations:

background-image: url("image.jpg");
background-repeat: no-repeat;
background-position: center;
background-attachment: fixed;


some values can be combined:

background: center fixed no-repeat url("image.jpg");


IMPORTANT:

Individual background properties are often easier to read
while learning.

The shorthand becomes useful once you understand what each
individual property controls.


============================================================
20. BACKGROUND PROPERTIES - MENTAL MODEL
============================================================

background-image
→ WHAT image is displayed


background-size
→ HOW LARGE the image is


background-repeat
→ WHETHER the image tiles


background-position
→ WHERE the image appears


background-attachment
→ HOW the image behaves while scrolling


background
→ shorthand that can combine background settings


============================================================
21. CSS GRADIENTS
============================================================

A gradient is a gradual transition between two or more
colors.


Two important CSS gradient functions are:

linear-gradient()

radial-gradient()


Gradients are generated by CSS.

You do NOT need a separate image file.


============================================================
22. LINEAR-GRADIENT()
============================================================

A linear gradient transitions colors along a straight line.


Basic example:

section {
    background-image: linear-gradient(blue, white);
}


This creates a transition between blue and white.


------------------------------------------------------------
GRADIENT DIRECTION
------------------------------------------------------------

You can control the direction.


Example:

linear-gradient(to right, blue, white);


Other examples:

to left

to bottom

to top

to bottom right


You can also use angles.


Example:

linear-gradient(45deg, blue, white);


Mental model:

linear-gradient()
→ colors transition along a line


============================================================
23. COLOR STOPS
============================================================

A gradient can contain more than two colors.


Example:

linear-gradient(red, yellow, green);


You can also control where a color occurs.


Example:

linear-gradient(red, yellow 40%, green);


The percentage is a COLOR STOP.


Color stops give you more precise control over where
transitions occur.


Mental model:

colors
+
positions
=
gradient appearance


============================================================
24. RADIAL-GRADIENT()
============================================================

A radial gradient expands outward from a point.


Example:

section {
    background-image: radial-gradient(white, blue);
}


It can radiate in circular or elliptical shapes.


Example:

radial-gradient(circle, white, blue);


Mental model:

linear-gradient()
→ transition follows a line


radial-gradient()
→ transition expands outward from a point


============================================================
25. BACKGROUND ACCESSIBILITY
============================================================

Background design affects readability and accessibility.


The foreground content must remain easy to distinguish
from the background.


This is especially important when text appears over:

- photographs
- gradients
- patterns
- strong colors


============================================================
26. COLOR CONTRAST
============================================================

Foreground text and its background need sufficient
contrast.


WCAG contrast guidance covered in the review:

Normal text:

minimum contrast ratio = 4.5:1


Large text:

minimum contrast ratio = 3:1


Low contrast can make text difficult or impossible to read
for many users.


============================================================
27. TEXT OVER BACKGROUND IMAGES
============================================================

Background photographs can contain both light and dark areas.

This can make one text color readable in one area but
unreadable in another.


Possible solutions include:

- choosing a less visually busy image
- choosing better foreground/background colors
- positioning text over a clearer part of the image
- placing a solid or semi-transparent layer behind text
- avoiding unnecessary background images altogether


Main principle:

The background should support the content.

It should not interfere with readability.


============================================================
28. ESSENTIAL INFORMATION AND BACKGROUNDS
============================================================

Background images are primarily presentational.

Do not rely on a decorative background alone to communicate
important information that users need to understand the
page.


Essential information should normally be represented in
the actual page content.


============================================================
29. BORDERS
============================================================

Borders create a visible boundary around an element.


A border commonly has three characteristics:

width
style
color


Example:

border: 3px solid blue;


Meaning:

3px
→ width

solid
→ style

blue
→ color


============================================================
30. BORDER SHORTHAND
============================================================

border is shorthand for the border's:

width
style
color


Example:

img {
    border: 3px solid blue;
}


General form:

border: WIDTH STYLE COLOR;


============================================================
31. INDIVIDUAL BORDER SIDES
============================================================

Each side can be styled independently.


Properties:

border-top
border-right
border-bottom
border-left


Examples:

img {
    border-top: 3px solid blue;
    border-right: 2px solid red;
    border-bottom: 4px dashed green;
    border-left: 1px dotted orange;
}


Each side can have a different:

width
style
color


============================================================
32. BORDER-STYLE
============================================================

border-style determines the visual appearance of the border.


Common values include:

solid
dashed
dotted
double


Examples:

border-style: solid;

border-style: dashed;

border-style: dotted;

border-style: double;


A visible border generally needs a border style other than
none.


============================================================
33. BORDER-RADIUS
============================================================

border-radius rounds an element's corners.


Example:

img {
    border: 3px solid darkgreen;
    border-radius: 15px;
}


Larger values produce more rounded corners.


Example:

border-radius: 25px;


border-radius can be used on many elements, including:

images
cards
buttons
containers


============================================================
34. BORDER VS OUTLINE
============================================================

A border participates in the element's box.

An outline is drawn around the outside of the element and
does not change the element's box dimensions.


Example:

img {
    border: 3px solid blue;
}


Example:

img {
    outline: 3px solid blue;
}


Mental model:

border
→ part of the box


outline
→ drawn around the box


Outline is also commonly used to create visible focus
indicators for interactive elements.


============================================================
35. BACKGROUND VS CONTENT IMAGE
============================================================

A CSS background image is decorative/presentational.

Example:

section {
    background-image: url("mountains.jpg");
}


An HTML img element represents actual page content.

Example:

<img src="mountains.jpg"
     alt="Snow-covered mountains beneath a cloudy sky">


Use the approach appropriate to the purpose of the image.


============================================================
36. QUICK BACKGROUND REFERENCE
============================================================

background-image
→ chooses the image


background-size: cover
→ fills the element; image may crop


background-size: contain
→ fits whole image; empty space may remain


background-repeat: repeat
→ tiles image


background-repeat: no-repeat
→ displays image once


background-position
→ controls alignment/location


background-attachment: scroll
→ background scrolls normally


background-attachment: fixed
→ background remains fixed to viewport


background
→ shorthand


